{"title":"2022 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 19","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2022 AMC 10B #19</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2022 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 18\">2022 AMC 12B #18</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_Alternate\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution Alternate</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Remark\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remark</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn.org_(Using_Logic_and_Casework)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn.org (Using Logic and Casework)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Interstigation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Interstigation</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Each square in a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/7/197151a067f49bd4daa1952d1a4fac322409f0a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 \\times 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> grid is either filled or empty, and has up to eight adjacent neighboring squares, where neighboring squares share either a side or a corner. The grid is transformed by the following rules:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Any filled square with two or three filled neighbors remains filled.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Any empty square with exactly three filled neighbors becomes a filled square.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>All other squares remain empty or become empty.</li></ul>\n<p>A sample transformation is shown in the figure below.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/3/00333423a7ddd483233c145023df02bdb469a3b9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]         import geometry;         unitsize(0.6cm);          void ds(pair x) {             filldraw(x -- (1,0) + x -- (1,1) + x -- (0,1)+x -- cycle,mediumgray,invisible);         }          ds((1,1));         ds((2,1));         ds((3,1));         ds((1,3));          for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 5; ++i) {             draw((0,i)--(5,i));             draw((i,0)--(i,5));         }          label(&quot;Initial&quot;, (2.5,-1));         draw((6,2.5)--(8,2.5),Arrow);          ds((10,2));         ds((11,1));         ds((11,0));          for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 5; ++i) {             draw((9,i)--(14,i));             draw((i+9,0)--(i+9,5));         }          label(&quot;Transformed&quot;, (11.5,-1)); [/asy]\" width=\"398\" height=\"182\" />\nSuppose the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/7/197151a067f49bd4daa1952d1a4fac322409f0a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 \\times 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> grid has a border of empty squares surrounding a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d278803d72fcc493afb1559174a483aa7f41d143.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> subgrid. How many initial configurations will lead to a transformed grid consisting of a single filled square in the center after a single transformation? (Rotations and reflections of the same configuration are considered different.)\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/3/253c2691ed603d5d600e2b1becee941d5268c900.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]         import geometry;         unitsize(0.6cm);          void ds(pair x) {             filldraw(x -- (1,0) + x -- (1,1) + x -- (0,1)+x -- cycle,mediumgray,invisible);         }          for (int i = 1; i &lt; 4; ++ i) {             for (int j = 1; j &lt; 4; ++j) {                 label(&quot;?&quot;,(i + 0.5, j + 0.5));             }         }          for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 5; ++i) {             draw((0,i)--(5,i));             draw((i,0)--(i,5));         }          label(&quot;Initial&quot;, (2.5,-1));         draw((6,2.5)--(8,2.5),Arrow);          ds((11,2));          for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 5; ++i) {             draw((9,i)--(14,i));             draw((i+9,0)--(i+9,5));         }          label(&quot;Transformed&quot;, (11.5,-1)); [/asy]\" width=\"398\" height=\"182\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/0/cc04fa4d612e3cb256e4e121720b3fbfeb920e61.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 14 \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 18 \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 22 \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 26 \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"397\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are two cases for the initial configuration:\n</p>\n<ol style=\"margin-left: 1.5em;\">\n  <li>The center square is filled. </li><p>\nExactly two of the eight adjacent neighboring squares of the center are filled. Clearly, the only possibility is that the squares along one diagonal are filled, as shown below:</p><p>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/9/8d9ca05cb188cd4c1903e7c6ced4882797e1db84.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import geometry; unitsize(0.6cm);  void ds(pair x) { \tfilldraw(x -- (1,0) + x -- (1,1) + x -- (0,1)+x -- cycle,mediumgray,invisible); }  ds((1,3)); ds((2,2)); ds((3,1));  for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 5; ++i) { \tdraw((0,i)--(5,i));     draw((i,0)--(i,5));     }  label(&quot;$2$ Configurations&quot;, (2.5,-1)); [/asy]\" width=\"148\" height=\"185\" />\nIn this case, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> possible initial configurations. All rotations and reflections are considered.</p><p class=\"mw-empty-elt\"> \n  </p><li>The center square is empty.</li><p>\nExactly three of the eight adjacent neighboring squares of the center are filled. The possibilities are shown below:</p><p>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/f/9af0bc0ac9bf851c3078feeda3696d8c643a3b6d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import geometry; unitsize(0.6cm);  void ds(pair x) { \tfilldraw(x -- (1,0) + x -- (1,1) + x -- (0,1)+x -- cycle,mediumgray,invisible); }  ds((1,3)); ds((3,3)); ds((1,1));  for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 5; ++i) { \tdraw((0,i)--(5,i));     draw((i,0)--(i,5));     }  ds((10,3)); ds((12,3)); ds((11,1));  for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 5; ++i) {     draw((9,i)--(14,i));     draw((i+9,0)--(i+9,5));     }  ds((19,3)); ds((20,1)); ds((21,2));  for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 5; ++i) {     draw((18,i)--(23,i));     draw((i+18,0)--(i+18,5));     }  ds((28,3)); ds((29,1)); ds((30,1));  for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 5; ++i) {     draw((27,i)--(32,i));     draw((i+27,0)--(i+27,5));     }      label(&quot;$4$ Configurations&quot;, (2.5,-1)); label(&quot;$4$ Configurations&quot;, (11.5,-1)); label(&quot;$4$ Configurations&quot;, (20.5,-1)); label(&quot;$8$ Configurations&quot;, (29.5,-1)); [/asy]\" width=\"912\" height=\"185\" />\nIn this case, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/6/7a6309944db1b047f8490d83eae974a2713d8177.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4+4+4+8=20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"147\" height=\"15\" /> possible initial configurations. All rotations and reflections are considered.</p><p class=\"mw-empty-elt\">\n</p></ol>\n<p>Together, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/6/e065c8e443f05a356b11631f3e8dcc57f8b64704.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2+20=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 22}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"144\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~mathboy100 ~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_Alternate\">Solution Alternate</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution Alternate\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Key Idea:\n</p><p>Only the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> x <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> inner grid can initially contain filled squares, since the outer border is empty. We want exactly the center square to be filled after one step.\n</p><p>Case 1: The center is initially filled.\n</p><p>A filled square remains filled precisely when it has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> filled neighbors. We examine the possible choices of filled neighbors of the center and require that no other square becomes filled.\n</p><p>This gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> valid configurations, counting rotations/reflections.\n</p><p>Case 2: The center is initially empty.\n</p><p>For the center to become filled, it must have exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> filled neighbors. There are several possible shapes for these three neighbors. Counting the configurations of each shape gives\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/6/ee6435098b377fa3ea809f950d46e2679097c95d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 + 4 + 4 + 8 = 20.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"152\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>The total number of configurations is\n</p><p>\\begin{align*}\n2+4+4+4+8\n&amp;=\\boxed{22}.\n\\end{align*}\n</p><p>Therefore the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/9/5e9001e3dce02f575858dafe0fb3a08449480a7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 22}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"71\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User:Lunar_paradox&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"User:Lunar paradox (page does not exist)\">Lunar paradox</a> (<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User_talk:Lunar_paradox&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"User talk:Lunar paradox (page does not exist)\">talk</a>) 18:06, 24 August 2026 (EDT)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remark\">Remark</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Remark\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This problem is based off of Conway's Game of Life, a cellular automaton created by British mathematician John Conway in 1970. The game follows the exact same pattern of rules as this problem, but is played on an infinite grid of cells.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn.org_.28Using_Logic_and_Casework.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn.org_(Using_Logic_and_Casework)\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn.org (Using Logic and Casework)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn.org (Using Logic and Casework)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/UYTiP3u5qE4\">https://youtu.be/UYTiP3u5qE4</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/CL_xjeRR02U\">https://youtu.be/CL_xjeRR02U</a>\n</p><p>~MathProblemSolvingSkills.com\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/JVDlHCSPF6k\">https://youtu.be/JVDlHCSPF6k</a>\n</p><p>~Hayabusa1\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Interstigation\">Video Solution by Interstigation</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Interstigation\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/gsaD0wQPVgY\">https://youtu.be/gsaD0wQPVgY</a>\n</p><p>~Interstigation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\